{"chunk_id": "bccee68d619d", "text": "The chapter describes the splendor of Yudhishthira\u2019s royal assembly, where many renowned kings, warriors of the Vrishni race, Gandharvas, and celestial musicians gather to honor the Pandavas. Their presence signals Yudhishthira\u2019s growing prestige and legitimacy as a ruler. Into this court arrives the celestial sage Narada, portrayed as a master of the Vedas, philosophy, politics, logic, and the sciences of governance and war. After being respectfully received and worshipped by Yudhishthira, Narada delivers an extensive series of probing questions about kingship. He asks whether the king governs justly, manages wealth properly, protects subjects, maintains secrecy in counsel, pays troops fairly, appoints wise ministers, supports agriculture and trade, avoids corruption, and restrains his own senses. The passage becomes a comprehensive manual of ideal statecraft, presenting a moral and administrative blueprint for righteous rule.\nThe sage Narada arrives at Yudhishthira\u2019s court and delivers an extensive interrogation on ideal kingship, governance, ethics, and statecraft.\nThemes: ideal kingship, statecraft and governance, moral duty, royal administration, sage counsel", "vector": [0.014231305569410324, -0.017109697684645653, 0.005512455012649298, -0.00553923100233078, -0.0006656281184405088, 0.019640004262328148, -0.02427220717072487, -0.013675708323717117, -0.026748962700366974, -0.009866859763860703, 0.017765702679753304, -0.015878012403845787, -2.3876559680502396e-06, 0.005268126726150513, -0.017069533467292786, -0.005559312645345926, 0.04549197852611542, -0.024968376383185387, 0.027418356388807297, -0.02341538295149803, 0.0011998878326267004, -0.0041970969177782536, -0.014204529114067554, 0.013950159773230553, -0.014579390175640583, 0.023428771644830704, 0.03620079904794693, -0.015918176621198654, -0.00485644955188036, -0.015770910307765007, -0.005314984358847141, -0.009023424237966537, 0.008347336202859879, -0.